ame><given-names>CM</given-names></name><name><surname>Georgiou</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Szutorisz</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Maia e Silva</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Pombo</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Barahona</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><name><surname>Dargelos</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Canzonetta</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Dillon</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Variant histone H3.3 marks promoters of transcriptionally active genes during mammalian cell division</article-title><source>EMBO Rep</source><year>2005</year><volume>6</volume><fpage>354</fpage><lpage>360</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15776021</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1038/sj.embor.7400366</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B26"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Trimborn</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Gribnau</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Grosveld</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Fraser</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Mechanisms of developmental control of transcription in the murine alpha- and beta-globin loci</article-title><source>Genes Dev</source><year>1999</year><volume>13</volume><fpage>112</fpage><lpage>124</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9887104</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B27"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wijgerde</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Grosveld</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Fraser</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Transcription complex stability and chromatin dynamics in vivo</article-title><source>Nature</source><year>1995</year><volume>377</volume><fpage>209</fpage><lpage>213</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7675106</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1038/377209a0</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B28"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>de Krom</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>van de Corput</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>von Lindern</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Grosveld</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Strouboulis</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Stochastic patterns in globin gene expression are established prior to transcriptional activation and are clonally inherited</article-title><source>Mol Cell</source><year>2002</year><volume>9</volume><fpage>1319</fpage><lpage>1326</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12086628</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/S1097-2765(02)00558-0</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><sec sec-type="display-objects"><title>Figures and Tables</title><fig position="float" id="F1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p><bold>Targeting of NF-E2p45 to chromatin compartment but displacing of GATA-1 from mitotic chromosomes in mitotic cells</bold>. Cells were stained with antibodies against proteins indicated on the left followed by FITC-conjugated secondary antibodies (Left) and PI (Center). Right is the merge of both images. Examples of interphase cells and mitotic cells of different stages from MEL cells are shown.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2199-8-31-1"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Flow cytometry analysis of asynchronous (A) and mitotic (M) MEL cells.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2199-8-31-2"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F3"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p><bold>The occupancies of NF-E2p45 on mouse &#x003b1;-like and &#x003b2;-like globin gene clusters in asynchronous (A) and mitotic (M) MEL cells</bold>. A. The schematic presentation of mouse &#x003b1;-like and &#x003b2;-like globin gene clusters. Globin genes are shown in dark rectangle. The DNase I hypersensitive sites-HS26, HS21, HS8 of mouse &#x003b1;-globin locus and HS3, HS2, HS1 of mouse &#x003b2;-globin locus, which are analyzed in ChIP assay, are shown in arrow. B. The relative occupancies of NF-E2p45 at HS sites of globin clusters in A and M cell populations were analyzed after normalizing the DNA amount differences in A and M cell populations through DNA input (In-A and In-M).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2199-8-31-3"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F4"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p><bold>Flow cytometry analysis of asynchronous (A) and mitotic (M) MEL cells</bold>. Cells were stained with antibodies against histone modifications indicated on the left followed by FITC-conjugated secondary antibodies (Left) and PI (red) or DAPI (blue) (Center). Right is the merge of both images. Mitosis marker-H3 phosphorylation (A) and active histone modifications-H3 acetylation (B) and H4 acetylation (C), H3 K4 dimethylation (D) and K79 dimethylation (E) were observed on mitotic chromosomes by in situ immunofluorenscence.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2199-8-31-4"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F5"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p><bold>The comparative analysis of global histone modificartions in asynchronous (A) and mitotic (M) MEL cells through western blotting</bold>. A. Histones prepared from A and M cell extracts were electrophoretically separated by SDS-polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis and stained with Coomassie Blue. B. The whole level of H3 and H4 acetylation, H3-K4 dimethylation in M cells is decreased compared to that in A cells, but H3-K79 dimethylation is stable.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2199-8-31-5"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F6"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p><bold>The distribution of active histone modifications on mouse &#x003b1;-like and &#x003b2;-like globin gene clusters in asynchronized (A) and mitotic (M) MEL cells</bold>. A, B. The changes of H3 and H4 acetylation level on the distant regulatory elements of globin loci in A and M cell populations. C, D. The changes of H3-K4 and H3-K79 dimethylataion on the distant regulatory elements of globin loci in A and M cell populations. The relative level of histone modifications at HS sites of globin clusters in A and M cell populations were analyzed after normalizing the DNA amount differences in A and M cell populations through DNA input (In-A and In-M).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2199-8-31-6"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F7"><label>Figure 7</label><caption><p><bold>The distribution of active histone modifications on differently active gene promoters in asynchronized (A) and mitotic (M) MEL cells</bold>. A. H3 and H4 acetylation. B. H3-K4 and H3-K79 dimethylation Comparative. ChIP analyses were performed with the equivalent DNA input amounts of chromatin from asynchronous and mitotic cells, then analyzed by PCR (In-A and In-M). PCR primer pairs flank the promoter regions of transcriptionally active, competent and inactive genes. The relative level of histone modifications at promoters of the individual genes in A and M cell populations were analyzed after normalizing the DNA amount differences in A and M cell populations through DNA input (In-A and In-M).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2199-8-31-7"/></fig><table-wrap position="float" id="T1"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>The primers and primer sequences used in ChIP assay.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><td align="left">Primer name</td><td align="left">Primer sequences</td><td align="left">Products</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left">c-myc</td><td align="left">5'-CTGTCCTGGCTCGCAGATTGTAAG-3'<break/>5'-AGTGAGCGGACGGTTGGAAGAG-3'</td><td align="left">231 bp</td></tr><tr><td align="left">hsp70</td><td align="left">5'-GTCGCAGCCAATCAACATAAGGATG-3'<break/>5'-GAAAGAGGCAGGAAACAAGTGACGC-3'</td><td align="left">229 bp</td></tr><tr><td align="left">&#x003b2;-actin</td><td align="left">5'-GGACCGGCAACGAAGGAGCT-3'<break/>5'-GTCCACAAGGGCGGAGGCTAT-3'</td><td align="left">360 bp</td></tr><tr><td align="left">&#x003b1;-globin</td><td align="left">5'-TTTGGTTTGAGGGACTTGCTTC-3'<break/>5'-TCATTGGCTGGTTTGTGCTTG-3'</td><td align="left">210 bp</td></tr><tr><td align="left">&#x003b2;maj</td><td align="left">5'-ACCGAAGCCTGATTCCGTAGAGC-3'<break/>5'-GATGTCTGTTTCTGGGGTTGTGAGTC-3'</td><td align="left">269 bp</td></tr><tr><td align="left">7SK</td><td align="left">5'-ACAGTCGGCTACATAAGGTCACCAC-3'<break/>5'-AGCTTCGGGAGGGACGCACA-3'</td><td align="left">213 bp</td></tr><tr><td align="left">nef-3</td><td align="left">5'-TGATAAAGAACAGCGGACCCTACG-3'<break/>5'-AGTTCCAGACCTCTTGTCACCCG-3'</td><td align="left">228 bp</td></tr><tr><td align="left">albumin</td><td align="left">5'-CAGATGGCAAACATACGCAAGG-3'<break/>5'-GCAGAGCCGGAGACGAAGAG-3'</td><td align="left">246 bp</td></tr><tr><td align="left">HS26</td><td align="left">5'-CGAGCAGATAACTAAGCCAAGC-3'<break/>5'-TTAAGGTAACAGGCACAGGGTC-3'</td><td align="left">185 bp</td></tr><tr><td align="left">HS21</td><td align="left">5'-TGCCAGAATAACATCCGAGAACC-3'<break/>5'-GAGAGTCACTGATAGGAATGCCACC-3'</td><td align="left">171 bp</td></tr><tr><td align="left">HS8</td><td align="left">5'-CCTTATCTGTCCCGTCACTTGCTC-3'<break/>5'-ATTAATCTCGTCCATTCAGTTTCACC-3'</td><td align="left">250 bp</td></tr><tr><td align="left">HS3</td><td align="left">5'-TGTTTCCCTGATGAGGATTCAATGG-3'<break/>5'-CCCACACATGGTCATCTATCTGAGC-3'</td><td align="left">274 bp</td></tr><tr><td align="left">HS2</td><td align="left">5'-TTCCTACACATTAACGAGCCTCTGC-3'<break/>5'-AACATCTGGCCACACACCCTAAGC-3'</td><td align="left">279 bp</td></tr><tr><td align="left">HS1</td><td align="left">5'-GCCTTACATCCCTGGACAGATAAAC-3'<break/>5'-GTGACATTGTGAGAAACTGAGACCC-3'</td><td align="left">342 bp</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap></sec></back></article>